{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoFr5hXpcDDTbRP6SpUQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I haven't run anything yet. This command is on the destructive list (`bq-rm`), so I need your approval of this exact command first.\n\n**Command**\n```\nbq rm -r -f -d example-project-dev:staging_old\n```\n\n**What it does**\n- `-d` targets the dataset `staging_old` in project `example-project-dev`.\n- `-r` deletes every table, view, and routine inside it, along with the dataset itself.\n- `-f` skips the confirmation prompt, so nothing will ask again.\n\n**Can it be undone?**\n- Not simply. Deleted tables can sometimes be recovered through time travel, but only within the dataset's time-travel window, which is 7 days by default. That recovery only covers tables, and only if the dataset's retention settings haven't changed.\n- Once the dataset itself is deleted, you can't restore it with a single command. Recovering the tables means recreating the dataset with the same name and then restoring each table by hand.\n- Views, routines, and the dataset's access settings and metadata are not recoverable that way.\n\n**Before you confirm**\n- I haven't inspected the dataset. I can run `bq ls example-project-dev:staging_old` first to show what's in it, and `bq show` to show its labels and last-modified time, so you can check it really is the old staging copy.\n\nReply \"yes, run it\" to approve the command as written. Or tell me to list the contents first."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":932,"cache_read_input_tokens":19687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":932},"output_tokens":453,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0a24272d